title: SMART: Background Medium Scan
agents: linux
catalog: os/storage
license: GPL
distribution: check_mk
description:
 SAS drives read their whole surface on their own while idle. This is the
 background medium scan (BMS). The drive logs every sector it could not read
 and whether it could reassign or rewrite that sector with the data intact.

 This check shows the scan status, the scan progress and the number of
 completed scans. It reports the lifetime average time per scan and the time
 since the scan counter last went up. All times are drive power-on time. The
 drive does not log when a scan finished, so the check records the power-on
 time at which it sees the counter go up. Until it has seen that once, it
 shows the time as a lower bound.

 Medium errors in the scan log go CRIT when they are unrecovered and WARN
 when the drive recovered the sector, for 7 days of power-on time after the
 entry. Older entries are OK. An unrecovered sector on storage with
 redundancy (Ceph, ZFS, RAID) should be rewritten from the redundant copy,
 for example with a deep scrub.

 A status of "no scans active" means background scanning is off on the
 drive and gives WARN. A halted scan gives WARN or CRIT, depending on the
 cause.

 If {sg_modes} from the {sg3-utils} package is installed, the check also
 shows the BMS settings of the drive: enabled or not, the scan interval, the
 pre-scan setting and the minimum idle time before a scan starts.

 All states and the time levels can be changed with the rule
 "SMART Background Medium Scan".

item:
 The device path and the last 8 characters of the serial number
 (for example {/dev/sdc (2JG5LEHB)}).

discovery:
 One service is created for each drive that reports a background scan
 results log, usually SAS hard disks.
